Signals: every spawned job is a process group, so a signal reaches the job and every
shell under it and nothing else. A job installs make's fatal handlers itself, since a
parent started as a shell's background job has them ignored, and a job forked from a
served request drops the request's connection, which the request alone answers. The parent forwards its fatal signals to every live
group, and its exit terminates whatever is still live, so nothing outlives the run. A job
spawned in the foreground is handed the terminal for its life and the parent takes it
back on wait; a background job that reads the terminal stops instead of competing for it,
which wait reports, and amk_foreground hands it the terminal and continues it. That is
the shell's job control, with the make process as the shell. The stop needs nothing from
make's own reaping: the kernel stops the reader's whole group, the job leader with it. Lua exposes the foreground
option on amk.spawn, the stopped status from amk.wait, amk.kill over the group, and
amk.foreground.

